# Tidy: Teaching a Low-Cost Arm Household Chores from a Few Phone Videos, with Uncertainty-Driven Demo Requests and Per-Step Recovery

*Draft. Every number in this paper is regenerated from `results/` by `make paper`, and `bench/check_claims.py` fails the build otherwise. **Evidence stage: all numbers are `sim`** (MuJoCo digital twin of an SO-100-class arm, synthetic phone videos). No real-robot result exists yet: the hardware has not been bought (BLOCKERS B-001). The real sessions are fully scripted in `SESSION_QUEUE.md`.*

## Abstract

We ask whether a ~{{const.budget_usd}} robot arm can learn household tabletop chores from about {{const.n_videos}} phone videos of a person doing the chore, with no teleoperation, and whether letting the robot choose which extra video to request helps. Tidy turns each video into hand, object and contact tracks. It retargets the human grasps to a parallel gripper with object-centric targets and trains an ACT policy on the resulting pseudo-demonstrations. A bootstrap ensemble supplies uncertainty for demo requests, and a per-step failure detector triggers local retries. In a digital twin of the workspace, and at equal human teaching time, the policy learned from {{const.n_videos}} videos clears the desk (four items into a bin) in {{m4.video_10.all}} of trials (seen + unseen placements, {{const.seeds}} seeds). The teleoperation-trained policy reaches {{m4.teleop_1x.all}} ({{m4.diff.video10_teleop1x}}), or {{m4aug.teleop_1x_aug.all}} with its best validated training configuration. Teleop at four times the human time reaches {{m4.teleop_4x.all}} ({{m4aug.teleop_4x_aug.all}} augmented). Embodiment-aware retargeting is essential: raw hand trajectories give {{m4.video_raw.all}}, and dropping the object-centric targets gives {{m4.video_hand.all}}. Uncertainty-driven requests did **not** reliably beat random requests: the difference over rounds 1–5 was {{m5.diff.pooled.uncertainty_random}}. Learned failure detection with a retry of only the failed step raises full-chore success by {{m6.diff.recovery}}, while blind retries hurt. These are simulation results with a simulated human and a simulated operator. The paper is explicit about which conclusions could flip on real hardware.

## 1 Introduction

Low-cost arms such as the SO-100/SO-101 make manipulation research affordable, but teaching them still mostly means teleoperation: a leader arm, practice, and many demonstrations. A phone video of a person doing the chore is far cheaper to make. The question is whether it carries enough information for a robot with a different body, and whether the robot can steer the person toward the demonstrations it needs.

We study three questions, all at the `sim` stage in this draft:

1. **Video vs teleop at equal human time.** Does a policy trained only on retargeted pseudo-demonstrations from ~{{const.n_videos}} phone videos reach the success of a teleop-trained policy given the same number of human minutes (filming, set-up and resets are all counted)?
2. **Uncertainty-driven demo requests.** If the robot asks for the extra video it is most uncertain about, does it improve faster than with random or round-robin requests at the same number of videos?
3. **Per-step failure detection and recovery.** Does a learned detector that triggers a safe retreat and a retry of the failed object step raise full-chore success?

**Contributions** (stated conservatively, following the prior-work review in `docs/PRIOR_WORK.md`; no component is claimed to be first):
(i) an end-to-end, open, tested pipeline from phone video to an SO-100-class policy with no robot demonstrations, including a safety layer enforced in code;
(ii) an equal-human-time comparison against teleoperation, with every human second accounted for;
(iii) a closed loop in which the learner requests the next *human video* (not a teleop correction), compared against random and round-robin requests;
(iv) a per-step failure detector with retry, plus a detector-free retry baseline and an oracle upper bound;
(v) negative and diagnostic results that matter for anyone building this. Delta-action chunking with ACT-style temporal ensembling silently fails when the arm lags the demonstrator. Corrective augmentation hurt. Self-practice was not worth its supervision cost.

## 2 Related work

Full annotated review with verified citations: `docs/PRIOR_WORK.md`. **Learning from human video.** Phantom and Masquerade train robot policies from human videos with little or no robot data by editing or retargeting the human embodiment. ZeroMimic, X-Sim, HumanEgo and EgoZero distil skills from egocentric or web video. WHIRL combines human video with robot self-practice. EasyMimic is the closest work: it uses the same class of low-cost LeRobot arm and the same video-to-gripper idea, but it co-trains with robot data and has no active requests or recovery. **Retargeting and perception.** We follow the common thumb–index-to-parallel-jaw mapping (e.g., Phantom, EasyMimic). The perception stack the real system targets (HaMeR/WiLoR hands, SAM 2 masks, CoTracker tracks, FoundationPose, Depth Anything) is replaced in sim by colour/depth stand-ins and a simulated hand estimator with a measured error model. **Policies.** ACT (Zhao et al., 2023) and Diffusion Policy (Chi et al., 2023) on LeRobot. VLA fine-tuning (OpenVLA, π0, SmolVLA) is out of reach without a GPU (BLOCKERS B-002). **Active and interactive imitation.** DAgger-family methods, EnsembleDAgger, ThriftyDAgger and Diff-DAgger query for *action labels* using novelty or uncertainty. AMF chooses which task to request demonstrations for. We request whole new *human videos* at chosen placements. **Failure detection and recovery.** FAIL-Detect, Sentinel and VLM success detectors detect failures. Rewind-IL, Phase-Conditioned IL, FLARE and FAR retry or respawn locally. Our detector is deliberately simple (calibrated logistic regression on robot-observable step features) so that the retry policy, not the detector, is what is evaluated.

## 3 System

```
phone video ──► perception (hands, objects, mat markers, depth) ──► events + phases ──► program inference
                                                                             │                 │
                                                                             ▼                 ▼
                                               retargeting (grasp axis, width, approach, IK filter, smoothing)
                                                                             │
                                                                             ▼
             pseudo-demos (object-centric obs, cumulative TCP-target chunks) ──► ACT (bootstrap ensemble)
                                                                             │
     overhead camera ──► object poses ──► executor (ready pose → policy → per-step detector → retry/help)
                                                                             │
                                                  IK ──► SafeController (limits, box, e-stop, watchdog, proximity) ──► arm
```

**Hardware model.** A 5-DoF SO-100-class arm with a parallel jaw, closed-form IK, servo dynamics and gravity compensation (D-002, D-004), {{const.ctrl_hz}} Hz end-effector control and a TCP speed limit of {{const.tcp_speed}} m/s. A fixed overhead RGB-D camera sees a printed mat with a 5×6 grid and four corner markers (`hardware/mat.pdf`). Grasps hold through a jaw-containment test plus a soft weld (D-003, D-008), so grasp *precision* matters but grasp *force* is not modelled.

**Safety layer** (`safety/`, tested in CI). Joint position, velocity and effort limits; a Cartesian workspace box and no-go zones; a latching e-stop polled every tick; a watchdog that freezes the arm when the policy stalls; and a camera-based person-proximity pause. Drivers accept commands only with the SafeController's token, and the real robot can only be started through `scripts/supervised_session.py`, which requires typed operator-presence confirmation, re-confirmation every five minutes and a resolved `SAFETY_LOG.md`.

## 4 From phone video to robot pseudo-demonstrations

**Synthetic phone videos (sim stage).** A simulated person performs each chore with minimum-jerk reaches from their side of the table, oblique approaches, a pinch across the object's narrow axis, tremor, and a hand-held camera with shake (D-013). Hand keypoints come from a simulated HaMeR/WiLoR-class estimator: ground truth plus AR(1) camera-frame noise, emitted only when skin pixels are visible. Mean clip length for desk clearing is {{m3.desk_clearing.vid_s}}.

**Perception and phases.** Camera pose per frame comes from the mat markers (tracked PnP, D-014). Objects are segmented by colour class and back-projected with depth, and the hand from the estimator. Grasp and release events come from hand–object proximity, aperture and the release-height cue (D-015). The task program (which objects move, to which landmark, with what offset, and in which order) is inferred across demos by choosing the landmark that minimises the variance of the relative place position (D-016).

**Retargeting.** Grasp centre = thumb–index midpoint, jaw yaw = thumb–index axis, jaw opening = aperture minus fingertip thickness. Object-centric snapping moves grasp and place targets to the tracked object when the hand is within a few centimetres (D-020). The robot then descends vertically for the last approach and lifts to transit height before horizontal moves. An IK feasibility filter rejects segments with mostly unreachable waypoints, and arc-length re-timing runs at robot speed. Pseudo-demo goal features are relabelled with the inferred program, so training and runtime goals mean the same thing (D-022).

