## 5 Policy learning

**Observation and action** (D-005, D-024, D-025). Observations are object-centric and low-dimensional: target-object and goal positions relative to the TCP, object yaw (not observed for rotationally symmetric objects), class, size, and jaw opening/command. The absolute TCP position, jaw yaw and pitch are deliberately *not* observed. Pitch comes from the arm's kinematics. Actions are chunks of {{const.chunk}} future TCP targets, each expressed relative to the TCP at prediction time, plus jaw yaw and gripper command. Chunks are executed with ACT temporal ensembling. **Training.** ACT (CVAE + transformer decoder), {{const.steps}} steps, the same configuration and compute for every data source (Rule 6). Hyper-parameters were chosen on validation placements only, averaged over the video and teleop sources (D-026).

**Executor.** For each object step the executor runs a ready-pose primitive (open the jaw if closed, rise to transit height; D-023), perceives once, and runs the policy at {{const.ctrl_hz}} Hz until release or timeout. The same executor runs the simulated teleoperator, every learned policy, the recovery wrapper, and (via `hardware/real_env.py`) the real arm.

**Design findings that changed the outcome** (validation placements, single seed, `results/sim/dev_policy_design_desk_clearing.json`; items placed):

| Variant | Video policy | Teleop policy |
|---|---|---|
| per-tick delta chunks, full observation | {{dev.video_10|delta_rel}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|delta_rel}} |
| per-tick delta chunks, newest-chunk execution | {{dev.video_10|delta_rel_newest}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|delta_rel_newest}} |
| cumulative target chunks, full observation | {{dev.video_10|cumulative_rel}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|cumulative_rel}} |
| **cumulative + minimal observation (default)** | {{dev.video_10|cumulative_minimal (default)}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|cumulative_minimal (default)}} |
| default + 4 mm perception-noise augmentation | {{dev.video_10|default + obs-noise aug 4mm}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|default + obs-noise aug 4mm}} |
| default + keyframe corrective augmentation | {{dev.video_10|default + corrective aug}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|default + corrective aug}} |
| default, chunk 20 | {{dev.video_10|default, K=20}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|default, K=20}} |
| default, 2× training steps | {{dev.video_10|default, 6000 steps}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|default, 6000 steps}} |
| MLP (same obs/action) | {{dev.video_10|mlp (cumulative, minimal)}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|mlp (cumulative, minimal)}} |
| Diffusion Policy (same obs/action, same steps) | {{dev.video_10|diffusion policy (cumulative, minimal)}} | {{dev.teleop_1x|diffusion policy (cumulative, minimal)}} |
| default, goal features *not* relabelled | {{dev.video_10|default, no goal relabelling}} | — |

The key failure was this. With per-tick delta chunks, a chunk encodes the demonstrator's *timing*. Under the servo model the arm moves only part of each commanded step, so temporally ensembled chunks decelerated and descended several centimetres short of the object (traced in D-025). Expressing chunks as spatial targets fixed it.

## 6 Uncertainty-driven demo requests

A bootstrap ensemble of {{const.members}} ACT policies is rolled out in the digital twin on a pool of {{const.pool}} fresh candidate placements per round (training-side seeds, never evaluation placements). For every candidate and every object step we average the ensemble disagreement (std of the next TCP target across members) separately over the *pick* phase (jaw open) and the *place* phase (jaw closed). The request is the candidate whose worst (object, phase) has the highest disagreement. It is rendered as text plus a key frame with that object circled, e.g. *"{{m5.example_request}}"*. **Baselines** at the same number of videos: *random* (a random candidate from the same pool) and *round-robin* (cycle objects × mat regions). Every requested video costs the same human time (film + set-up + reset + {{const.request_overhead_s}} s to read the request).

## 7 Failure detection and recovery

After each object step the robot computes features it can observe: the jaw opening when the gripper was last commanded closed relative to the object width, lift ticks, timeout, and the camera-measured distance from the object to its goal, its height relative to the goal, how far it moved, and the step duration. Two class-balanced logistic regressions predict *grasped* and *placed*. The failure threshold is calibrated on held-out steps for ≥ 0.8 precision. On a flagged failure the robot retreats safely (opens the jaw, rises vertically), re-perceives, and retries that object step, up to {{const.max_retries}} times. It then asks for help. We compare against no recovery, a detector-free "retry every step once" baseline (are extra attempts alone enough?), and an oracle detector.

## 8 Evaluation

**Protocol** (frozen and checksummed, `eval/protocol/`). Per chore there are {{const.trials_seen}} seen-placement cards, {{const.trials_unseen}} unseen-placement cards (at least one object in a held-out grid cell), unseen-object substitutions, and lighting variants. Seed spaces for evaluation and training are disjoint by construction, and filming/teleop never use held-out cells. Each condition is repeated over {{const.seeds}} independent data collections + trainings. We pool trials and report Wilson {{const.ci}} intervals, and differences with Newcombe intervals. Scoring uses simulator ground truth at this stage. On the real robot it is the overhead-camera auto-score confirmed by the operator, with a {{const.audit_frac}} video audit.

**Human-time accounting** (Rule 5). Video: clip duration + {{const.setup_s}} s set-up + {{const.reset_s}} s reset per clip, and rejected clips still count. Teleop: demo duration + set-up + reset, with demos added until the budget of the {{const.n_videos}}-video condition is spent. The simulated operator moves at {{const.operator_speed}} of the robot's speed limit with tracking noise and occasional hesitation. A faster, more precise operator is a sensitivity condition. Self-practice charges the supervision time of every practice episode plus its reset.

