At equal human time, the 10-video policy beats teleop by {{m4.diff.video10_teleop1x}}. Giving the teleop baseline its own best validated configuration (corrective augmentation, which helps teleop but hurts video data; D-021) narrows but does not close the gap: {{m4aug.diff.video10_teleop1xaug}} at 1×. Video also still beats plain teleop given twice ({{m4.diff.video_10_teleop_2x}}) and four times ({{m4.diff.video_10_teleop_4x}}) the human time. Why: in the same minutes a person films {{const.n_videos}} clips but only about {{m4.teleop_1x.n_demos}} teleop demos (the simulated operator succeeded in {{m4.teleop_1x.operator_success}} of their demos). That amounts to {{m4.video_10.n_segments}} vs {{m4.teleop_1x.n_segments}} usable object-step segments. Retargeted video pseudo-demos are also cleaner (robot-speed, no hesitation, no tracking noise). The teleop policies were unstable across seeds (see per-seed results in the result file). **Ablations:** retargeting is essential ({{m4.diff.video_10_video_raw}} vs raw wrist trajectories), and so are object-centric targets ({{m4.diff.video_10_video_hand}}). Task-agnostic sim pre-training made no difference ({{m4.diff.video_simpre_video_10}}). One round of self-practice changed success by {{m4.diff.video_practice_video_10}} while roughly quadrupling the human time ({{m4.video_practice.min}} min, because a person must supervise and reset every practice episode). The robot's own camera-based auto-score agreed with ground truth on {{m4.video_practice.practice_agree}} of practice steps. Doubling the videos from 10 to 20 gives {{m4.diff.video_20_video_10}}. The −VLA-pretraining ablation was not run (no GPU).

