### 8.3 Failure detection and recovery

Detector on held-out eval-card steps: precision {{m6.det.test.prec}}, recall {{m6.det.test.rec}}, false-alarm rate {{m6.det.test.far}} ({{m6.det.test.n}} steps), and phase attribution correct on {{m6.det.test.phase_acc}} of detected failures.

| Mode | Full-chore success | Mean retries / trial |
|---|---|---|
| no recovery | {{m6.no_recovery}} | {{m6.no_recovery.retries}} |
| retry every step once (no detector) | {{m6.blind_retry_once}} | {{m6.blind_retry_once.retries}} |
| **learned detector + retry** | **{{m6.recovery}}** | {{m6.recovery.retries}} |
| oracle detector + retry | {{m6.oracle_recovery}} | {{m6.oracle_recovery.retries}} |

![M6](figures/m6_recovery.png)

Recovery vs none: {{m6.diff.recovery}}; vs blind retry: {{m6.diff.recovery_vs_blind}}; oracle vs none: {{m6.diff.oracle}}. The detector is what makes retrying useful. Retrying every step once without it is *worse* than no recovery ({{m6.diff.blind}}), because re-attempting an already-placed item often knocks it or others out of the bin. With the learned detector, recovery recovers a large part of the oracle's gain at fewer retries per trial.

