## 9 Safety

All runs go through the same SafeController as the real arm. In a dedicated audit we replayed trained policies and checked the *measured* arm state: {{safety.episodes}} episodes, {{safety.ticks}} control ticks, **{{safety.incidents}} limit violations** (workspace box, no-go zones, joint limits, joint and TCP speed). The filter intervened on {{safety.interventions}} ticks, which were mostly commands below the workspace floor. Peak TCP speed was {{safety.max_tcp_speed}} m/s against the {{const.tcp_speed}} m/s limit. CI fails if any limit can be exceeded or the e-stop path is removed from a control loop. No real-robot session has been run, so `SAFETY_LOG.md` is empty.

## 10 Limitations and threats to validity

* **Everything is simulated**, including the person, the phone, the hand-pose estimator (Gaussian error model) and the teleoperator. The video-vs-teleop gap depends on modelled operator speed and noise. A skilled leader-arm operator on real hardware may close it. The real comparison (session S2) is the decisive experiment.
* **Grasping is simplified** (containment test + weld), so real grasp failures from slip, compliance and mass are missing. Sim success likely overestimates real success for every method.
* **Perception stand-ins.** Colour segmentation stands in for SAM 2 + class prompts, and synthetic depth for phone LiDAR. The fingertip-cap fallback is weak ({{m3.sweep.fingertip_caps.f1}} event F1), so a real system needs a learned hand-pose model.
* **Small-scale learning.** Low-dimensional object-centric observations, no VLA, and CPU-only training. Policies are trained per chore.
* **Statistics.** Trials within a seed share one trained policy, so pooled Wilson intervals understate between-seed variance, which is large for teleop. Per-seed numbers are in the result files.
* **Active learning negative result.** It may depend on pool size, the ensemble size of {{const.members}}, and requesting 2 videos per round. We did not tune these on evaluation data.

## 11 Conclusion

At the sim stage, the answer to the main question is **mixed**. Learning from {{const.n_videos}} phone videos clearly beats teleoperation at equal human time in our twin, and the retargeting design choices are essential. Uncertainty-driven requests did not beat random or round-robin requests. Per-step recovery driven by a simple learned detector helps substantially, and blind retrying does not. All of it awaits the real-robot sessions queued in `SESSION_QUEUE.md`.

